<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chet Ton dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th–20th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Chao Chet Ton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chet Ton dynasty, meaning 'seven princes', was the ruling house of Lampang and Chiang Mai, descended from the seven sons of the Lampang leader Chai Kaeo. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila</a>, the brothers joined Siam in driving the Burmese out of Lan Na from 1774 and then governed Chiang Mai, Lampang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> as Siamese tributaries. The family kept the title of ruler of Chiang Mai until 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chet-ton-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chet Ton dynasty" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Chiang_Mai_(1802%E2%80%931899).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
